      },
      "source": [
        "###<b> Processing Images using Gaussian Blurring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HgkB_-J2KhBQ"
      },
      "source": [
        "###<b> Gaussian Blurring on train data"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "kKLEHTXur3_y"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Notes on Gaussian Blurring\n",
        "\n",
        "Gaussian Blurring is a common image processing technique used to reduce noise and detail in an image. It works by applying a Gaussian function to the image, effectively smoothing the image by averaging pixel values with their neighbors. This can be particularly useful in tasks like edge detection, image segmentation, or as a preprocessing step before applying machine learning algorithms.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "- kernel_size: The size of the Gaussian kernel. A larger kernel will result in more blurring. The size should typically be odd (e.g., (5, 5)).\n",
        "- sigma: The standard deviation of the Gaussian distribution. A value of 0 means that OpenCV will automatically calculate it based on the kernel size."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "U7GtZThUI6ug"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "def apply_gaussian_blur(images, kernel_size=(5, 5), sigma=0):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Apply Gaussian Blurring to a list of images.\n",
        "\n",
        "    Args:\n",
        "        images (numpy array): Array of images to be blurred.\n",
        "        kernel_size (tuple): Size of the Gaussian kernel. Defaults to (5, 5).\n",
        "        sigma (float): Standard deviation in X and Y direction. Defaults to 0 (auto).\n",
        "\n",
        "    Returns:\n",
        "        numpy array: Array of blurred images.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    blurred_images = []\n",
        "    for image in images:\n",
        "        blurred_image = cv2.GaussianBlur(image, kernel_size, sigma)\n",
        "        blurred_images.append(blurred_image)\n",
        "    return np.array(blurred_images)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "drR043sEr3_y"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Load images\n",
        "# Make sure the images are already loaded into train_images and test_images)\n",
        "train_images_blurred = apply_gaussian_blur(train_images, kernel_size=(5, 5), sigma=0)\n",
        "test_images_blurred = apply_gaussian_blur(test_images, kernel_size=(5, 5), sigma=0)\n"
      ]
    },
